# sandbox: a LAST-RESORT escape hatch for Dagger coding agents — run an argv
# in an arbitrary container ("sandbox") with the workspace mounted at a
# workdir.
#
# Coding agents normally get curated tools (checks, generators, editor tools);
# this module is for the rest: ad-hoc commands that need a real container.
# Session state lives in `let` fields (@cache(Never) + nonce cache-busting,
# per-call workspace copying), following the Map-state pattern of the staff
# module.
#
# Sandbox definitions are artifacts: any zero-arg Container-returning field of
# a workspace module (this module's own `demo`, `git` and `dang` included) has
# a DAG address like dag://sandbox/dang, and FindArtifacts(type: "Container")
# lists them. There is no catalog tool here; discovery is the engine's.
#
# Mechanics per session (a named entry in the private maps):
#   - `exec` copies the caller's workspace to /workspace in the sandbox
#     container, runs the argv there, and stores the resulting container
#     plus the workspace tree that was copied in (the "before" for change
#     extraction).
#   - `changes` extracts /workspace's drift from that before-tree as a
#     Changeset — the tool layer auto-applies a returned Changeset to the
#     workspace, so returning it IS delivery.
#   - Freshness rule: a CONTINUED session only re-copies the CURRENT
#     workspace when the sandbox holds no un-extracted /workspace changes;
#     otherwise the container tree is kept (so nothing is clobbered) and a
#     note points at `changes`. A dirty session whose tree already MATCHES
#     the current workspace (its extraction was applied) self-heals to
#     clean — see the drift check in exec.
#
# NOTE on the sandbox arg: it is typed Container! and resolved at the
# CALLER's boundary, never in this module. The tool layer lifts an address
# string — an image ref like "golang:1.26", a DAG address like
# "dag://<module>/<function>" naming a workspace module's Container
# artifact, or a Container ID from a prior result — against the session's
# bound workspace (core/llm_object_tools.go, liftableObjectArg), and the CLI
# does the same for --sandbox flags in workspace mode. That is what makes
# sibling module refs work: an in-module address() call would resolve in
# THIS module's schema, where installed workspace siblings are not
# demand-loadable (hack/designs/sandboxes.md §4, "Resolution context is the
# point").
#
# NOT registered in dagger.toml: install per workspace where wanted
# (hack/designs/sandboxes.md §3: not installed by default anywhere).

let workdir = "/workspace"
let dangVersion = "v2.1.3"
# Tail-truncate command output to keep tool results context-friendly.
let tailLines = 200

# Last `lines` lines of s, with a truncation marker when shortened.
let tail(s: String!, lines: Int! = tailLines): String! {
  let all = s.split("\n")
  if (all.length <= lines) {
    s
  } else {
    let marker = "… (" + toString(all.length - lines) + " earlier lines truncated) …"
    marker + "\n" + all.takeLast(lines).join("\n")
  }
}

# "" means the implicit session, kept under a reserved name.
let sessionName(session: String!): String! {
  if (session == "") { "last" } else { session }
}

# Build the sandbox's workspace like a local clone: reconstruct the checkout
# from the host git session, then overlay the pending workspace edits
# (Workspace.git.uncommitted: modified and untracked files, gitignored ones
# left out as git status would). This avoids syncing the entire host
# directory on each exec. Non-git workspaces retain the directory-copy
# behavior.
let workspaceTree(ws: Workspace!): Directory! {
  {
    let git = ws.git
    git.head.tree(discardGitDir: false, depth: 0, includeTags: true)
      .withChanges(git.uncommitted)
      .sync
  } rescue {
    e: Error => ws.directory("/")
  }
}

"""
Run ad-hoc commands in arbitrary containers with the workspace mounted — the
last-resort escape hatch when no curated tool fits.
"""
type Sandbox {
  # ---- session state --------------------------------------------------------
  # Keyed by session name. Map[...] cannot be exposed through module API, so
  # these stay private `let` fields; engine-object handles (Container,
  # Directory) serialize as IDs in module state and rehydrate on the next
  # call (the modules/staff pattern).

  # container as it was after the session's last exec
  let afters: Map[Container!]! = [:]
  # workspace tree copied in at session start / last refresh — the "before"
  # side of change extraction
  let befores: Map[Directory!]! = [:]

  """
  Compose the sandbox tools onto a base LLM.
  """
  agent(base: LLM!): LLM! @agent {
    # The sandbox definitions stay artifacts (dag://sandbox/dang), not tools:
    # they are reached by address as exec's `sandbox`.
    base
      .withTools(currentNode, except: ["demo", "git", "dang"])
      .withSystemPrompt(systemPrompt)
  }

  """
  A demo sandbox: plain alpine.

  Guarantees any workspace with this module installed one resolvable
  Container address ("dag://sandbox/demo"), and serves as the module-ref
  test target.
  """
  demo: Container! {
    container.from("alpine:3.20")
  }

  """
  A lightweight sandbox for Git operations.
  """
  git: Container! {
    container
      .from("alpine:3.20")
      .withExec(["apk", "add", "--no-cache", "git"])
  }

  """
  A sandbox with the Dang CLI installed.
  """
  dang: Container! {
    container
      .from("golang:1.25")
      .withExec(["go", "install", "github.com/vito/dang/v2/cmd/dang@" + dangVersion])
  }

  """
  Run an argv in a sandbox container with the workspace copied to /workspace.

  This is a LAST RESORT: prefer curated tools (checks, generators, editor
  tools) whenever one fits. The workspace is COPIED in, never mounted — the
  real workspace only changes when you pull the sandbox's edits back out
  with `changes`.
  """
  exec(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    The sandbox container to run in: an image ref like "golang:1.26", a
    sandbox definition by DAG address like "dag://sandbox/dang"
    (FindArtifacts(type: "Container") lists them), or a Container ID from a
    prior result. Ignored when continuing an existing session — the
    session's stored container is used — unless `fresh`.
    """
    sandbox: Container!,
    """
    The argv to run, e.g. ["sh", "-c", "ls -la"]. Run with expect: ANY — a
    nonzero exit is reported, not raised.
    """
    args: [String!]!,
    """
    Named session to continue or create; "" runs ephemerally (state is still
    tracked under the implicit name "last" so changes can be pulled).
    """
    session: String! = "",
    """
    Drop the stored session state first and start over from `sandbox`.
    """
    fresh: Boolean! = false,
  ): Sandbox! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): running a command is a side effect; a cached replay
    # would silently skip it. The nonce env var busts the exec cache
    # underneath for the same reason.
    let name = sessionName(session)
    if (fresh) {
      self.afters = self.afters.without(name)
      self.befores = self.befores.without(name)
    }

    let current = workspaceTree(ws)

    # The container to exec in, and the "before" tree in effect for this
    # session after this call.
    let ctr = container
    let sessionBefore = current

    let stored = self.afters[name]
    if (stored != null) {
      # Continued session. Only re-copy the CURRENT workspace when the
      # sandbox holds no un-extracted /workspace changes; blindly re-copying
      # would clobber work the caller hasn't pulled yet.
      let prevBefore = self.befores[name]
      if (prevBefore == null) {
        raise `invariant violated: session "${name}" has a container but no before-tree`
      }
      let pending = stored.directory(workdir).changes(prevBefore)
      if (pending.isEmpty) {
        # withoutDirectory first: withDirectory overlays, so files deleted
        # from the workspace since the last copy would otherwise linger.
        ctr = stored
          .withoutDirectory(workdir)
          .withDirectory(workdir, current)
        sessionBefore = current
      } else {
        # Self-healing for agent mode: `changes` advances the baseline on
        # ITS returned value, but a Changeset-returning tool does not rebind
        # the toolset object (core/llm.go: only a tool returning the bound
        # object's own type does), so the advance is lost and the session
        # would look dirty forever. When the sandbox tree already matches
        # the CURRENT workspace — the extracted changes were applied — the
        # session is de-facto clean: re-copy and move on.
        let drift = stored.directory(workdir).changes(current)
        if (drift.isEmpty) {
          ctr = stored
            .withoutDirectory(workdir)
            .withDirectory(workdir, current)
          sessionBefore = current
        } else {
          print(`note: session "${name}" has un-extracted changes under ${workdir}; keeping its tree (the current workspace is NOT re-copied). Call changes(session: "${name}") to pull them.`)
          ctr = stored
          sessionBefore = prevBefore
        }
      }
    } else {
      # New session: the sandbox arrives already resolved — the tool layer
      # (or the CLI) lifted the caller's address string into a Container at
      # the caller's boundary (see NOTE at top).
      ctr = sandbox
        .withDirectory(workdir, current)
        .withWorkdir(workdir)
    }

    let after = ctr
      # bust Dagger's exec cache so each call actually re-runs
      .withEnvVariable("SANDBOX_EXEC_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withExec(
        args,
        expect: ReturnType.ANY,
        experimentalPrivilegedNesting: true,
      )

    let code = after.exitCode
    if (code != 0) {
      print("exit code: " + toString(code))
    }
    let stdout = after.stdout
    if (stdout.trimSpace != "") {
      print(tail(stdout))
    }
    let stderr = after.stderr
    if (stderr.trimSpace != "") {
      print("stderr: " + tail(stderr))
    }

    self.afters = self.afters.with(name, after)
    self.befores = self.befores.with(name, sessionBefore)

    let delta = after.directory(workdir).changes(sessionBefore)
    if (!delta.isEmpty) {
      # .{{path}} first: .length can't be called directly on a GraphQL
      # object list.
      let n = delta.diffStats.{{path}}.length
      print(`sandbox changed ${toString(n)} file(s) under ${workdir} — call changes(session: "${name}") to pull them into the workspace`)
    }

    self
  }

  """
  Extract a session's /workspace drift as a Changeset. Returning it delivers
  it: the tool layer applies a returned Changeset to the workspace.
  """
  changes(
    """
    Session to extract from; "" means the implicit "last" session.
    """
    session: String! = "",
  ): Changeset! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    let name = sessionName(session)
    let after = afters[name]
    if (after == null) {
      raise `no sandbox session "${name}" — run exec first (sessions: ${names})`
    }
    let before = befores[name]
    if (before == null) {
      raise `invariant violated: session "${name}" has a container but no before-tree`
    }
    let afterTree = after.directory(workdir)
    # Advance the baseline so an already-extracted diff is not re-applied by
    # a later `changes`. KNOWN LIMIT (see final spike notes): this mutation
    # only persists where the caller keeps the returned object — in a
    # `dagger call ... exec ... changes` chain the Changeset is terminal, and
    # in agent mode a non-Sandbox-returning tool does not evolve the bound
    # toolset state.
    self.befores = self.befores.with(name, afterTree)
    afterTree.changes(before)
  }

  """
  List live sandbox sessions and whether each holds un-extracted changes.
  To find sandbox definitions, use FindArtifacts(type: "Container").
  """
  listSessions: String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    if (afters.isEmpty) {
      return "(no sandbox sessions)"
    }
    afters.map { name, after =>
      let before = befores[name]
      let state = if (before == null) {
        "?"
      } else {
        let d = after.directory(workdir).changes(before)
        if (d.isEmpty) {
          "clean"
        } else {
          `${toString(d.diffStats.{{path}}.length)} file(s) pending — call changes(session: "${name}")`
        }
      }
      `${name}: ${state}`
    }.values.join("\n")
  }

  let names: String! {
    if (afters.isEmpty) {
      "(none)"
    } else {
      afters.keys.join(", ")
    }
  }

  let systemPrompt: String! = """
    You have a sandbox escape hatch: `exec` runs a command in any container
    with the workspace copied to /workspace. It is a LAST RESORT, and almost
    always the wrong first move:
    - Tests and lints: use checks — sandbox a test runner only when checks
      can't select what you need (e.g. one test via -run) and the iteration
      saving is significant.
    - Codegen and formatting: use generators.
    - Anything a module tool covers: use the module tool — it returns
      structured results a raw command doesn't.

    Mechanics:
    - `sandbox` is an address: an image ref like "golang:1.26", or a sandbox
      definition by DAG address like "dag://sandbox/dang".
      FindArtifacts(type: "Container") lists the definitions in scope.
    - The workspace is COPIED in, not mounted. Nothing you do in the sandbox
      touches the real workspace until you call `changes`, which extracts
      /workspace's drift as a Changeset and applies it.
    - Pass `session` to keep state (installed packages, files outside
      /workspace) across calls; `fresh: true` starts a session over.
      `listSessions` shows live sessions and their pending changes.
    - A continued session only sees your latest workspace edits when it has
      no un-extracted /workspace changes — pull pending changes first.

    Reaching for exec is a signal: if no tool covered the need, note it; and
    if you exec the same setup twice, graduate it into a sandbox definition —
    a Container-returning function on a module (an existing one that owns
    the concern, or a workspace-local `sandboxes` module you create) — then
    use its address.
  """
}
